// A memoization is a claim about renders, and most of them are unearned. `useMemo` and
// `useCallback` cost a dependency array that has to stay correct and a reader who has to
// verify it, and they buy nothing unless something downstream reads the identity they
// preserve. Two things earn one: a computation expensive enough to feel — not an object
// literal, a `find` over a dozen items, or a parse of a handful of fields — and
// referential identity a real dependency consumes, which means an effect's dep list, a
// memoized child, another hook's dep list, or a hook's returned API whose callers hold it
// in one of those. A survivor names that dependency in a one-line comment beside it;
// anything else is deleted and the value computed inline.
//
// `useRef` is not matched: it holds identity that outlives the render rather than caching
// one, so there is nothing for it to earn.
//
// Each call shape is spelled out twice, with and without a type argument. GritQL matches
// a call snippet field by field, so `useMemo($...)` misses `useMemo<Item[]>(…)` — the
// type argument is a field that snippet does not have — and the annotated form is how a
// memo that returns a list tends to be written.
//
// Warn rather than error, because the justification lives at the consumer and the pattern
// only sees the call. A survivor carries it as the suppression's reason —
// `// biome-ignore lint/plugin: <what consumes the identity>` — which is how the rule
// reads clean with the earned memos still in place.
//
// Scoped by biome.json to `apps/web/src` and `shared/ui`, source only: a spec renders
// hooks rather than writes them. The mobile surface adds its own scope.
language js

or {
  `useMemo($...)` as $hook,
  `useCallback($...)` as $hook,
  `useMemo<$t>($...)` as $hook,
  `useCallback<$t>($...)` as $hook,
  `$_.useMemo($...)` as $hook,
  `$_.useCallback($...)` as $hook,
  `$_.useMemo<$t>($...)` as $hook,
  `$_.useCallback<$t>($...)` as $hook
} where {
  register_diagnostic(span=$hook, message="This hook has to earn its place — an expensive computation, or referential identity a real dependency consumes (an effect dep, a memoized child, a hook's returned API). Keep it only with a `// biome-ignore lint/plugin:` comment beside it naming that dependency; otherwise delete it and compute the value inline.", severity="warn")
}
